DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     Skein is a new family of cryptographic hash functions based on the
     Threefish large-block cipher.  Its design combines speed, security, sim‐
     plicity, and a great deal of flexibility in a modular package that is
     easy to analyze.  Skein is defined for three different internal state
     sizes—256 bits, 512 bits, and 1024 bits—and any output size.  This allows
     Skein to be a drop-in replacement for the entire SHA family of hash func‐
     tions.

     The SSKKEEIINN225566__IInniitt(), SSKKEEIINN225566__UUppddaattee(), and SSKKEEIINN225566__FFiinnaall() functions
     are the core functions.  Allocate an _S_K_E_I_N_2_5_6___C_T_X, initialize it with
     SSKKEEIINN225566__IInniitt(), run over the data with SSKKEEIINN225566__UUppddaattee(), and finally
     extract the result using SSKKEEIINN225566__FFiinnaall(), which will also erase the
     _S_K_E_I_N_2_5_6___C_T_X.

     SSKKEEIINN225566__EEnndd() is a wrapper for SSKKEEIINN225566__FFiinnaall() which converts the re‐
     turn value to a 33-character (including the terminating '\0') ASCII
     string which represents the 256 bits in hexadecimal.

     SSKKEEIINN225566__FFiillee() calculates the digest of a file, and uses SSKKEEIINN225566__EEnndd()
     to return the result.  If the file cannot be opened, a null pointer is
     returned.  SSKKEEIINN225566__FFiilleeCChhuunnkk() is similar to SSKKEEIINN225566__FFiillee(), but it
     only calculates the digest over a byte-range of the file specified,
     starting at _o_f_f_s_e_t and spanning _l_e_n_g_t_h bytes.  If the _l_e_n_g_t_h parameter is
     specified as 0, or more than the length of the remaining part of the
     file, SSKKEEIINN225566__FFiilleeCChhuunnkk() calculates the digest from _o_f_f_s_e_t to the end
     of file.  SSKKEEIINN225566__DDaattaa() calculates the digest of a chunk of data in
     memory, and uses SSKKEEIINN225566__EEnndd() to return the result.

     When using SSKKEEIINN225566__EEnndd(), SSKKEEIINN225566__FFiillee(), or SSKKEEIINN225566__DDaattaa(), the _b_u_f
     argument can be a null pointer, in which case the returned string is al‐
     located with malloc(3) and subsequently must be explicitly deallocated
     using free(3) after use.  If the _b_u_f argument is non-null it must point
     to at least 33 characters of buffer space.

     The SKEIN512_ and SKEIN1024_ functions are similar to the SKEIN256_ func‐
     tions except they produce a 512-bit, 65 character, or 1024-bit, 129 char‐
     acter, output.

